# TODO - Ongoing compatibility assurance and product hardening

## Objective

Continuously find and fix product defects and improve usability, tests,
working Linux/Firefox personal delivery, and macOS/Safari/ChatGPT compatibility
without blocking
functional delivery. Keep this record open until explicitly closed by Alberto.

## Acceptance

**ONGOING -- NEVER AUTO-COMPLETE.** This final task is a continuously open
maintenance and product-improvement lane. Its acceptance criterion is **IT WORKS
better after each iteration**: find a real issue or useful improvement,
reproduce it where practical, add focused regression coverage, fix or improve
the implementation, and verify that existing behavior still works. Repeat this
cycle whenever development agents are authorized to work; the existence of this
TODO does not itself start a background agent or grant deployment authority.

Give provider-change detection unusually high and recurring maintenance
priority. Blooket is an external platform that can change between product
releases, silently invalidating previously working browser selectors, React
controls, routes, authentication states, transport payloads, timing assumptions,
and success/read-back signals. Allocate dedicated, reviewable commits to
compatibility probes, fixture drift detection, regressions and fixes, not just
new features.

Continuously evolve the test matrix even when the existing tests are green;
green fixtures from an older provider build are not proof the live integration
still works.

For every observed provider UI/API change or unexplained write/read failure:
record the observed browser/product build and affected contract, add a minimal
sanitized failing fixture or read-only smoke test, distinguish a real provider
change from an application regression, update the adapter only after review,
and validate ordinary, interrupted and ambiguous outcomes. Keep both positive
and fail-closed negative tests: changed or missing controls, hydration/order,
identity/session/navigation, overlays, question types/timing/media, and remote
persistence after reload. No credentials, captured private content, cookies,
or volatile provider action tokens belong in tracked fixtures.

Run `npm run provider:drift:check` for provider-facing source changes and
keep expanding its sanitized synthetic cases. Periodically perform
owner-authorized read-only browser compatibility checks against the actual
connected provider session. Keep opt-in and human stops for account
mutations, challenges, browser permissions and unfamiliar access states. A drift
signal must prevent unsafe writes or honestly mark verification inconclusive;
it must never trigger automatic selector guessing, security bypass, or replay
of ambiguous mutations.

Document evidence, failures and unresolved risks with each
compatibility-focused commit. This is ongoing intensive maintenance, not a
finite box to check or a requirement to block safe feature delivery.

Focus on bugs, missing tests, correctness, recovery, edge cases, performance,
behavior, EN/ES UX, accessibility, UI polish, Firefox/Safari/ChatGPT
compatibility, **theoretical macOS ARM64 compatibility assurance** from
official docs, contracts, portable tests and opt-in native CI, and reliable
Linux x64 user installations and native checks. Keep Mac-only concerns here;
never claim a real recipient-Mac
or native behavior was tested when it was only inferred.

**Manual closure gate:** do NOT mark this record completed, move it to
`docs/todo/completed/`, or remove it from `TODO.md` unless Alberto explicitly
instructs closing **this exact ongoing task**. Passing tests, fixing known bugs,
shipping an app, or completing the other TODOs does not satisfy that gate.
Remain active indefinitely until that explicit instruction, even with zero
currently known bugs. Never block functional delivery or safe releases solely
because this ongoing improvement queue remains open.

Keep provider challenges, consent, secret boundaries, honest verification, and
safe rollback intact. This task can discover and correct issues in previously
completed areas without reopening their milestones; never self-approve a
release, modify accounts without authorization, or weaken existing safeguards
just to obtain green tests.

## Compatibility coverage guidance

Use current official Apple, WebKit, GitHub Actions, and ChatGPT/MCP
documentation. Deterministic implementation, protocol tests, and native ARM64
CI are sufficient engineering evidence when manual interaction adds no distinct
fact.

The opt-in CI path is linear: general validation runs first on Linux. Only after
it passes does macos-arm run on macos-26, require ARM64, build the single
supported Mac package, compile the Safari WebExtension host, and exercise the
packaged localhost UI through Safari WebDriver.

Keep production Apple trust, signing/notarization, Gatekeeper, installed
Safari behavior, native Keychain and login-item behavior, filesystem
normalization, and ChatGPT-specific compatibility here. Also own Safari-only
editor acceptance such as native chooser/clipboard permission behavior,
eyedropper interaction, accessibility/responsive behavior, and animated preview
replay. Do not reopen earlier records merely to demand a recipient Mac, Safari,
or ChatGPT smoke test.

A CAPTCHA, unfamiliar login, organization choice, consent prompt, browser
permission, or other genuine human-validation boundary still requires the human
when it occurs. That is an execution checkpoint, not a standing platform
blocker.

## Implementation notes

Keep general CI portable and platform-neutral. The macOS ARM job owns only
platform-specific build, Safari conversion, native package behavior, and Safari
WebDriver smoke coverage; Release owns only release gating and publication.

The supported Mac product architecture is ARM64. Intel/x64 is not a package,
update target, CI target, or release asset. Long-term client behavior for the
completed personal-skill system, native settings/Keychain behavior, and
ChatGPT-specific OAuth registration/reauthorization behavior also live only in
this final record.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-15, a content-free authorized Chrome read found one same-origin
HTTP 429 resource response on the provider Edit route during initial page
loading. This is not a Save receipt, nor proof that the later Save was
throttled. A dedicated isolated-world diagnostic now records a monotonic
timestamp before
a single Save click and only classifies HTTP 429 fetches to that exact same
Edit route after the attempt.

Earlier hydration 429s, unrelated set IDs, foreign initiators, replaced
documents and unowned latches are refused.
Synthetic provider-drift tests prove the positive and negative shapes; an
uncertain attempted write remains journaled without auto-retry.

The browser mutation host now separates temporarily missing document-origin
evidence after its own navigation from absent or malformed identity on a
teacher-selected existing tab. These cases cannot be polled into authority;
synthetic tests assert zero modal opens and zero Save attempts.

Dedicated read/navigation drift tests now cover a same-route document
replacement during Chrome's final `tabs.get`, temporary loss of question-card
controls after an owned Cancel, and persistent or malformed post-Cancel
results. They fail closed without untrusted adoption or another Save.
The live Chrome check reported compatible Edit selectors, not a new saved
question receipt; the user journals and unsaved editor were preserved.

A same-day read-only Chrome 155 structural check against the existing
Typing editor still found the expected numeric timer, visible prompt and
Save Question control. The current Blooket provider build was not identifiable
from this observation. The canonical authenticated session read succeeded but
My Sets and capability reads remained unconfirmed while a local unsaved editor
was present. The journal remained untouched; this compatibility signal alone
is not a remote publication receipt.

See
`docs/technical/provider-change-detection.md` for the scoped evidence.

On 2026-10-14, an explicit `provider:drift:check` command grouped the native
Blooket page and extension-host regressions. It passed 290 synthetic tests
before adding a separate read-only shape inspector for the question editor.
The inspector's fixture cases distinguish a valid Typing/Multiple Choice form
from renamed answer slots, missing selectors, changed time controls, malformed
JSON, wrong set identity and missing or duplicated fields.

Later red-to-green cases added semantic drift: invalid correct answers, match
modes, time limits, numbering, randomization and media-field types. Security
and organization overlays produce human stops. No fixture uses personal account
content or claims a confirmed remote Save.

An authorized read-only Chrome DevTools observation of an existing unsaved
Typing editor reported compatible structural controls and matching visible and
provider-serialized text. It did not reload, click, create, adopt a journal
receipt or prove server persistence. The final inspector's native read-only
contract and incident workflow are documented in
`docs/technical/provider-change-detection.md`.

On 2026-10-14, the installed ClangFormat 23.1.2 rejected a read-only
`--dry-run --Werror` of the existing native C exchange helper against its
committed Jig C formatting profile. ClangTidy also exited nonzero, with 101
error diagnostic lines under the committed warnings-as-errors profile.
These profiles cannot truthfully be marked active and passing in Jig merely
because the executables exist. The native C17 compiler syntax gate remains
green; formatter/linter adoption requires a separate reviewed correction.

On 2026-10-14, nine previously untracked Markdown, YAML, Clang and
TypeScript lint profiles became committed, reviewed repository assets.
Jig 26.3.0 refused enabling executable ClangFormat/ClangTidy without
native validation capabilities and root configs; its reviewed TypeScript
linter expected a tooling-only root tsconfig incompatible with the
currently authoritative application tsconfig.

These executors remain
explicitly disabled in `linters.toml`, not silently reported active.
Strict `npm run check`, native C compiler checks, and zero-diagnostic
Jig validation remain in force. Activation awaits a pinned executable
validation contract and a separately admitted tooling scope; a stored
profile is not a passing external lint run.

Current GitHub-hosted runner documentation identifies macos-26 as an ARM64
standard runner. setup-node v7 supports pnpm cache restoration, and Apple
documents safaridriver --enable for Safari WebDriver automation.

On 2026-10-08, live Chrome inspection reproduced contradictory Quizzes
copy: the heading promised automatic publication while the notice reported
an unimplemented adapter. EN/ES text now describes available draft preparation
and labels the pending feature as Blooket publication rather than connection.
No publication gate was enabled or browser transport reclassified by this copy
change.

The same EN/ES browser check exposed a stale empty-draft message after changing
language. Its generated paragraph now participates in ordinary translation,
preserving draft contents while updating the surrounding product message.

On 2026-10-08, the owner confirmed that native Mac/Safari checks stay in this
ongoing record rather than blocking P2/P3 completion. Native visual checks must
also confirm that MCP commands, service startup, and updater restart do not
open Terminal windows. Functional implementation and portable safety/recovery
evidence remain required in their owning records.

The completed lifecycle record supplies a compiled native main-app wrapper,
owned `SMAppService` login agent, and explicit local controls with actual OS
approval status. P4 retains Swift compilation, signed bundle execution, login /
reboot single-instance behavior, Keychain, owned unregister/uninstall cleanup,
and visual confirmation that no Terminal opens. Portable fixtures prove the
controller and product composition only; they never stand in for these native
observations or reopen the completed functional milestone.

